ve 13 + 1 = 14 tiles, but one is used to form a meld with two from hand.\n\nSo I remove two from hand (T3, T4), add the claimed T2 to form the meld.\n\nHand now has 13 - 2 = 11 tiles.\n\nThen I must discard one, ending with 12 tiles in hand and one exposed meld.\n\nBut a complete hand needs four melds and a pair, or seven pairs, etc.\n\nCurrently no melds.\n\nAfter this Chi, I will have one exposed chow, and 12 tiles in hand.\n\nMy hand after Chi and before discard: [W2, W3, W6, B1\u00d72, B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, B9]\n\nB7\u00d73 is a concealed pung.\n\nB1\u00d72 and B8\u00d72 are pairs.\n\nW2, W3, W6 are singles.\n\nB9 is single.\n\nNow, evaluate whether to Chi or Pass.\n\nEvaluation:\n\nOption 1: Chi T3 (i.e., claim T2 to form [T2,T3,T4])\n\nPros:\n- Forms a chow, progressing toward a full hand.\n- Removes T3 and T4, which are relatively isolated.\n- Keeps flexibility with other suits.\n\nCons:\n- Exposes a meld, making it harder to get concealed patterns.\n- The chow [T2,T3,T4] is not particularly valuable.\n- I have a strong chance of forming a pung-based hand with B7 (already a pung) and potential with B1 or B8.\n\nMoreover, I have three B7s \u2014 a concealed pung worth at least 2 points (Dragon Pung? No, B7 is Bamboo-7, not a dragon. Dragon tiles are J1,J2,J3.\n\nBamboo-7 is a suited tile.\n\nPung of terminal or honor: B7 is terminal (7,8,9 are not terminals; terminals are 1 and 9). So B7 is not terminal.\n\nSo the B7 pung currently scores nothing by itself.\n\nBut it can be part of patterns.\n\nLook at bamboo: I have B1\u00d72, B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, B9\u00d71.\n\nThat's promising for a lower/higher split or flush.\n\nSpecifically, B7,B8,B9 \u2014 I have B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, B9\u00d71 \u2014 very close to a chow of B7,B8,B9 and a pair of B8, or even better.\n\nIf I draw B9, I can have B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, B9\u00d72 \u2014 then I can keep two pairs and a pung.\n\nOr if I draw B6, I can form a chow B6,B7,B8.\n\nBut I already have a pung of B7, so breaking it might not be good.\n\nBetter to keep the pung and aim for a hand around bamboo.\n\nAlso, W2,W3 \u2014 could be part of a chow W1,W2,W3 or W2,W3,W4.\n\nI have W2,W3,W6 \u2014 W6 is isolated.\n\nT3,T4 \u2014 weak.\n\nBy doing Chi, I commit to a mixed hand with a chow in bamboos, but it's early.\n\nI have discarded T1 and T9 \u2014 so I'm getting rid of terminals in dots and characters? T1,T9 are bamboos.\n\nI discarded T1 and T9, which suggests I'm not keeping a bamboo hand, but I have B7,B8,B9 \u2014 wait, B is dots, T is bamboos.\n\nClarify:\n- W: Characters (\u4e07)\n- T: Bamboos (\u6761)\n- B: Dots (\u997c)\n\nSo:\n- I have B-tiles (dots): B1\u00d72, B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, B9\u00d71 \u2014 so dots suit.\n- T-tiles (bamboos): T3, T4 \u2014 only two.\n- W-tiles (characters): W2, W3, W6\n\nI discarded T1, T9 \u2014 these are bamboos (not dots).\n\nSo I discarded some bamboos, but kept dots.\n\nB is dots, so B1 is Dot-1, etc.\n\nSo my strong suit is dots (B): I have B1\u00d72, B7\u00d73, B8\u00d72, B9\u00d71.\n\nThat's 8 dot tiles: two B1, three B7, two B8, one B9.\n\nVery promising for a full flush in dots.\n\nPossible patterns:\n- Full Flush (24 points) if all melds in dots.\n- Lower Tiles (if I focus on B1) or Upper Tiles (B7,B8,B9).\n- I have B7\u00d73 \u2014 pung of upper dot.\n- B8\u00d72 \u2014 pair.\n- B9 \u2014 singleton.\n- B1\u00d72 \u2014 pair.\n\nIf I can get another B9, I have a pair, or B6/B8 to form chows.\n\nW2,W3 \u2014 could form W1,W2,W3 or W2,W3,W4.\n\nT3,T4 \u2014 could form T3,T4,T5.\n\nBut if I go for Full Flush, I need to eliminate non-dot tiles.\n\nCurrently, I have W2,W3,W6 (3), T3,T4 (2) \u2014 5 non-dot tiles.\n\nToo many.\n\nBut if I can convert some.\n\nChi [T2,T3,T4] uses up T3 and T4, removing two non-dot tiles, and adds a bamboo chow \u2014 but that's a different suit, so now I have a mixed hand.\n\nAfter Chi, I will have an exposed chow in bamboos, and my hand is mostly dots, but with character tiles W2,W3,W6.\n\nSo I would have a half-